## Mandatory DNAM Branding & Format Integrity

Your license to distribute, embed, or link any part of the software in any
hardware device, firmware, audio plugin, or software application is strictly
conditioned on all of the following:

1. **User-Facing DNAM Branding (No White-Labeling):** Any hardware device,
   embedded firmware, closed-source or open-source audio plugin, or software
   application that links, embeds, or runs `dnam-engine` (or its bindings) or
   loads `.dnam` models must visibly credit **"DNAM"** ("Dynamic Neural Audio
   Modeler") or **"Powered by DNAM"** wherever the modeling engine, effect
   block, or model loader is shown to users (including plugin faceplates or
   headers, hardware block selectors, model or preset browsers, "About" screens,
   and product documentation). You must not white-label, rebrand, obscure, or
   market `dnam-engine` or the DNAM modeling architecture under another name.
2. **Container, Signature & Watermark Integrity (No Circumvention):** Any
   product, plugin, script, or tool that uses, links, or is based on the
   software—or that loads, saves, creates, or exchanges DNAM models—must keep
   the unmodified `.dnam` and `.dnam-ckpt` file extensions, magic headers
   (`DNAM` and `DNCK`), and schema identifiers
   (`"architecture": "DNAM_TriPhysics"`). You must not:
   - modify the software or re-wrap `.dnam` or `.dnam-ckpt` files to output or
     load a renamed, disguised, or incompatible file extension or container
     format;
   - strip, alter, forge, or bypass creator attribution or provenance
     (`metadata.author`, `metadata.license`, `metadata.provenance`),
     cryptographic signatures (`creator_signature`, `marketplace_receipt`), or
     device-authorization checks (`ENCRYPTED_ARENA`, `authorized_devices`);
   - issue or sign `marketplace_receipt` certificates without a written
     marketplace license from the licensor; or
   - remove, wash, or tamper with buyer watermarks in the binary weight arenas
     (`Arena 0..2`).
3. **Required Notices:** You must ensure that anyone who gets a copy of any
   part of the software from you (in source or binary form, including within
   product documentation or "About" notices of hardware devices and plugins)
   also gets a copy of these terms, as well as copies of any plain-text lines
   beginning with `Required Notice:` that the licensor provided with the
   software. For example:

> Required Notice: Copyright 2026 Peter Nguyen <peter@phn.dev>

